Z||000001||Dave Gunsul||07-30-2001||02:07 AM||||I know you meant this for Archie but Archie has installed a 383 before.<BR>It was a 383 with dual quads and NOS!<BR>So the answear is yes you can but only if you let me test drive it.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P>------------------<BR>Dave Gunsul<BR>N.I.F.E. member<BR>86 GT modified<BR>85 SE daily driver||||reg||14
Z||000002||afRaceR||07-30-2001||02:13 AM||||I know you have to run a 400 harmonic balancer on 383's built from a 350 block and 400 internals but I thought they were the same size as the 350's. the 350's are internally balanced, 400's are externally. If the 400 balancer is bigger and keeps you from using it, you can have the crank internally balanced and just use the 350 balancer. <BR>If archie doesn't get back to you, I got a 377 built on the shelf waiting a future project I can check the blancers on it for you.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000003||Archie||07-30-2001||08:01 AM||||Yes, you can use the 400 crank in a Fiero V-8 swap. You will have to have your engine builder balance your rotating assembly with my Flywheel and HBPulley.<P>Archie||||reg||1
